{
  "$comment": "Config keys of every block type the Traceworks engine runs: key, type, allowed values, legacy value, required. Read by the analyzer (BlockConfigSchema, ConfigKeyRules). It holds no editor texts and no definitions (the stage shapes of compress, which the analyzer checks itself, see CompressRules), no list of compress stage ops and no block type Traceworks does not run; execution_mode is limited to ai and shell. A string value \"#name\" in a field list refers to sharedFields.name. \"type\" (one name or a list: boolean, integer, string, array, object, null), \"items\" (the type of a list's entries), \"minimum\" and \"maximum\" are the types and ranges the run accepts: the analyzer reports any other value as invalid-config-value, and the run reads every setting through BlockSettings, which fails the block on it (BlockConfigSchemaTest holds the ranges against BlockSettings and, for compress and compress-ai, against their executors). A null value counts as absent. \"fields\" of an object key (validation) are its keys, checked like a block's. \"default\" is not read. \"requiredOneOf\" = exactly one of these keys must be set (shell: command or container_args). \"legacy\" = old value still accepted, \"legacyFor\" = old key name of another key, \"ignoreCase\" = the executor compares case-insensitively, \"onlyWith\" = the executor reads the key only when these other keys have these values (\"*\" = the key is set). \"topLevelOnly\" = the key works only on a top-level block, not nested in a switch case (the analyzer flags it there, and a requiredOneOf partner becomes required). Whoever teaches an executor a new config key adds it here.",
